// A response — the type this package's fault vocabulary has been describing and which nobody wrote.
//
// `./fault.wac` declares `ResponseFault` as `union<RequestFault, BadStatus>` and argues carefully
// about what a *response* can get wrong that a request cannot. There was no `Response`. Ten members
// of vocabulary for a type the package does not have, which is the shape `../../../QUESTIONS.md`
// counts as *137 union members that nothing discriminates*, arriving one level up: **the vocabulary
// for a thing can be finished before the thing exists, and nothing notices.**
//
// Written now because `@/packages/server/src/routes.wac` needed one, which is this directory's usual
// order and the reason it works.

import { Vec, Bytes } from "core";
import { Headers } from "./headers.wac";

/**
 * What a handler answers.
 *
 * The shipped `Response { i32 status; Headers headers; u8[] body; }` with two changes, both from
 * writing the routes rather than from reading the type.
 */
export struct Response {
  /**
   * **A `Status`, not an `i32`** — see below. The shipped `reason(i32)` is a nineteen-arm `if` chain
   * mapping code to phrase, which is a lookup keyed on a value from a closed set.
   */
  Status status;
  Headers headers;
  /**
   * **`Bytes`, not `u8[]`**, so a handler answering a slice of the request it was given does not copy.
   * `@/packages/server/src/routes.wac`'s echo route returns a view of its own input twice.
   */
  Bytes body;

  Response of(Status status) { return Response(status, Headers.create(), Bytes.empty()); }

  /** Add a field. Framing headers are `write`'s to add, which is the shipped rule and a good one. */
  void set(this, string name, string value) {
    this.headers.push(name.toBytes(), value.toBytes());
  }
}

/**
 * The status codes this system answers, and **the reason phrase is a property of the arm.**
 *
 * The shipped file has `reason(i32 status)`, nineteen `if`s returning a string, and a caller writes
 * `Response.create(400)`. Two things go wrong with that and both are in the tree: a code with no
 * phrase falls through to a default, and nothing stops `Response.create(4000)`.
 *
 * An enum with a method is the same table with the key checked. It is deliberately **not** every code
 * in RFC 9110 — the ask is *what this system answers*, and a closed set is only worth having if it is
 * closed. `Other(i32 code)` is the escape and it is the one arm with no phrase, which is honest:
 * a code nobody enumerated has no phrase anybody agreed on.
 */
export enum Status {
  Ok, Created, NoContent,
  MovedPermanently, NotModified,
  BadRequest, Forbidden, NotFound, MethodNotAllowed, RequestTimeout,
  PayloadTooLarge, HeaderFieldsTooLarge, UnsupportedMediaType,
  InternalServerError, NotImplemented, ServiceUnavailable, VersionNotSupported,
  Other(i32 code)

  /** The wire number. */
  i32 code(const this) {
    return match (this) {
      Ok: 200, Created: 201, NoContent: 204,
      MovedPermanently: 301, NotModified: 304,
      BadRequest: 400, Forbidden: 403, NotFound: 404, MethodNotAllowed: 405, RequestTimeout: 408,
      PayloadTooLarge: 413, HeaderFieldsTooLarge: 431, UnsupportedMediaType: 415,
      InternalServerError: 500, NotImplemented: 501, ServiceUnavailable: 503,
      VersionNotSupported: 505,
      Other(c): c,
    };
  }

  /** Decoration — the code is what a client reads. Empty for `Other`, which is the point. */
  string reason(const this) { … }
}

/** The response on the wire. Framing is decided here and not by the handler. */
export Bytes write(const Response r, bool headOnly) { … }

// ## 1. `Status` is the third enum-over-a-closed-i32 in this directory and the first with an escape
//
// `@/packages/tor/src/pathsel.wac` turned four states of two booleans into an enum;
// `@/packages/datetime`'s `Sign` turned `""`/`"+"`/`"-"` into three arms. Both sets are genuinely
// closed. HTTP status codes are not: the registry has sixty-odd and a private one is legal, so an
// enum that tried to be complete would be wrong tomorrow.
//
// `Other(i32 code)` is what makes the enum honest, and it costs the thing the enum was for: a caller
// can write `Other(404)` and get a `NotFound` that does not equal `NotFound`. **An open set spelled
// as a closed one with an escape hatch has two spellings for every member the hatch can reach**, and
// nothing here can forbid the second — the constructor would have to refuse the seventeen codes the
// other arms already name, which is a runtime check in a place with no runtime.
//
// The shipped `i32` has one spelling and no check. This has a check for the sixteen that matter and
// two spellings for those sixteen. Neither is right and the difference is worth stating rather than
// declaring a winner: **you can have one spelling or a checked set, and an open registry gives you
// the choice and not both.**
//
// ## 2. What could not be written
//
// **`reason()` is empty for `Other` and the type does not say so.** Every other arm answers a
// non-empty string; `Other` cannot, because a code nobody enumerated has no agreed phrase. So
// `reason()` is `string` where it wants to be `string?` for one arm out of seventeen, and making it
// `string?` costs every caller an unwrap for a case that arises once. Second instance of *a total
// function that is partial on one arm*, after `@/packages/datetime`'s `Take.Accept`.
//
// **`Bytes body` can outlive the request it is a view of.** The reason it is `Bytes` is that the echo
// route answers a slice of its input; the risk is that a handler stores the `Response` and the buffer
// is reused. `core/slice.wac` says a slice cannot dangle, so this is retention rather than safety —
// and `@/packages/json/src/value.wac` made the *opposite* call for the same reason, owning its
// strings so a tree does not pin a document. **Two files, one question, opposite answers, and both
// are right**: a response is written and dropped, a tree is kept. Nothing in the type says which kind
// of value it is, and that is the whole of the difference.
//
// **`write` takes `headOnly` and the reason is `HEAD`.** A response to `HEAD` carries the headers of
// the `GET` and no body, so the flag is *the request's method, remembered*. It is a parameter because
// `Response` does not know what asked for it — the same missing pairing as
// `@/packages/box/src/cp.wac`'s `Side`.
//
// This said *the fifth time a caller has had to hand back something it already told the callee once*.
// Enumerated 2026-09-05: **seven**, and they are three different problems that the count merged.
//
//   * **The callee could compute it.** `@/packages/ts/src/report.wac`'s `say(const Blame b,
//     BundleFault f)` — `b` is `blameFor(f)`. Pure redundancy, and `say(User, NoPrefix(3))`
//     type-checks. A code smell, not a language gap.
//   * **The value already carries it, for most members.** `@/packages/box/src/cp.wac`'s
//     `say(string path, FileFault f)` — and **seven of `FileFault`'s ten members have a `path`
//     field.** The caller supplies it because the other three (`Unsupported`, `OtherFault`,
//     `NotGranted`) do not, so a union whose members *almost* agree on a field makes every caller
//     carry that field alongside. A fault-design smell, and a new one: the fix is to give the three
//     the field or to split the union, not to change the language.
//   * **Nothing has it.** `write(r, headOnly)`, `policy(Side, FileFault)`,
//     `@/packages/git/example/ignorelint.wac`'s `say(const Rules, const Finding)`,
//     `@/packages/tor/src/verdict.wac`'s `say(string what, Invalid why)`. Four, and only these are
//     the missing-pairing question.
//
// So the ask is four sites rather than seven, and the two that dropped out are each a different
// thing worth fixing. Fifth bare ordinal enumerated in two days and the fifth to be wrong.
